('127.413.d.8.B',3,'Rotation of an aircraft about its vertical axis is called:','["yaw", "thrust", "roll", "pitch"]'::jsonb,0,'Yaw turns the nose left or right.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.B',4,'Rotation about the lateral axis, which raises or lowers the nose, is:','["roll", "pitch", "drag", "yaw"]'::jsonb,1,'Pitch tilts the nose up or down.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.C',3,'The primary control surface used to control roll is the:','["rudder", "flap", "aileron", "elevator"]'::jsonb,2,'Ailerons bank the aircraft.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.C',4,'The elevator controls motion about which axis?','["yaw axis", "vertical stabilizer", "roll axis", "pitch axis"]'::jsonb,3,'The elevator changes pitch.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.D',3,'The four forces acting on an aircraft in flight are lift, weight, thrust, and:','["drag", "friction heat", "buoyancy", "tension"]'::jsonb,0,'Drag opposes thrust.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.D',4,'In straight and level, unaccelerated flight, thrust is equal to:','["weight", "drag", "pressure", "lift"]'::jsonb,1,'Forces are balanced in equilibrium.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.E',3,'Weight is the force of gravity acting on an object''s:','["volume", "length", "mass", "color"]'::jsonb,2,'W = m x g.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.E',4,'An object has a mass of 10 kg. Using g = 9.8 m/s2, its weight is:','["10 N", "9.8 N", "980 N", "98 N"]'::jsonb,3,'10 x 9.8 = 98.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.F',3,'A pilot pulled upward in a tight turn feels a g-force greater than 1 g, which makes the pilot feel:','["heavier", "weightless", "colder", "lighter"]'::jsonb,0,'Acceleration increases apparent weight.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.F',4,'Weight is a force due to gravity, whereas g-force is a measure of:','["mass", "acceleration relative to gravity", "density", "volume"]'::jsonb,1,'G-force compares acceleration to 1 g.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.G',3,'In straight and level flight, the free-body diagram shows lift pointing up and weight pointing:','["forward", "backward", "down", "up"]'::jsonb,2,'Weight acts toward Earth.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.G',4,'In a level turn, the lift vector is tilted, so its vertical component must equal the aircraft''s:','["speed", "thrust", "drag", "weight"]'::jsonb,3,'Vertical equilibrium holds in a level turn.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.H',3,'Increasing engine power changes the amount of:','["thrust", "gravity", "mass", "pressure altitude only"]'::jsonb,0,'Throttle controls thrust.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.H',4,'Extending flaps on landing increases both lift and:','["weight", "drag", "thrust", "mass"]'::jsonb,1,'Flaps add camber and drag.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.I',3,'The wing, fuselage, and tail are examples of major:','["engines", "instruments only", "aircraft components", "fuels"]'::jsonb,2,'These are structural components.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.I',4,'The vertical stabilizer with its rudder helps control:','["pitch", "power", "roll", "yaw"]'::jsonb,3,'The rudder yaws the aircraft.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.J',3,'Aerodynamics is the study of how air moves around:','["objects", "plants", "colors", "words"]'::jsonb,0,'It is a subset of aerospace engineering.','original'),
('127.413.d.8.J',4,'Aerodynamics applies to aircraft, rockets, and also to:','["only trains", "cars and buildings", "only birds", "only boats"]'::jsonb,1,'Air flows around many objects.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.A',3,'A wing generates lift in part because air flowing over the curved top surface has lower:','["color", "mass", "pressure", "temperature only"]'::jsonb,2,'Pressure differences create lift.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.A',4,'A wing also generates lift by deflecting air downward, an effect described by Newton''s:','["periodic law", "law of gases", "first law of thermodynamics", "third law"]'::jsonb,3,'Action and reaction.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.B',3,'Increasing an airfoil''s angle of attack, within limits, makes lift:','["increase", "stay zero", "decrease", "vanish"]'::jsonb,0,'More angle produces more lift up to stall.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.B',4,'Angle of attack is the angle between the wing''s chord line and the:','["horizon", "relative wind", "runway", "fuselage"]'::jsonb,1,'AoA is relative to airflow.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.C',3,'On a lift coefficient versus angle of attack chart, the curve rises and then drops sharply after the:','["landing", "takeoff", "stall angle", "cruise"]'::jsonb,2,'The peak is maximum CL.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.C',4,'The highest point on a CL versus AoA curve represents:','["zero lift", "minimum drag", "the runway length", "the maximum lift coefficient"]'::jsonb,3,'Maximum CL occurs at the stall angle.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.D',3,'A stall occurs when the airflow over the wing:','["separates and lift drops sharply", "speeds up", "becomes smoother", "adds power"]'::jsonb,0,'Flow separation causes the stall.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.D',4,'To recover from a stall, a pilot should reduce the:','["airspeed indicator", "angle of attack", "wing area", "thrust forever"]'::jsonb,1,'Lowering the nose restores flow.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.E',3,'Drag caused by the shape of an object moving through air is called:','["lift", "thrust", "form drag", "gravity"]'::jsonb,2,'Form drag is shape-related pressure drag.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.E',4,'Drag caused by air rubbing along a surface is:','["induced drag only", "weight", "wave drag only", "skin friction drag"]'::jsonb,3,'Surface friction creates drag.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.F',3,'When a wing is tilted to a steeper angle of attack, its drag generally:','["increases", "decreases to zero", "stays zero", "flips sign"]'::jsonb,0,'Higher AoA brings more drag.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.F',4,'Induced drag is a by-product of producing:','["weight", "lift", "thrust", "heat"]'::jsonb,1,'Lift creates wingtip vortices.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.G',3,'A drag coefficient chart plots CD against:','["passenger count", "altitude only", "angle of attack", "fuel"]'::jsonb,2,'CD varies with AoA.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.G',4,'On a CD versus AoA chart, the lowest point shows the angle with minimum:','["thrust", "weight", "lift coefficient", "drag coefficient"]'::jsonb,3,'The minimum is the lowest drag.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.H',3,'If drag increases during flight and thrust stays the same, the aircraft will:','["slow down", "climb", "stay the same", "speed up"]'::jsonb,0,'Net force becomes negative.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.H',4,'Higher drag in cruise reduces range because the engine must burn more:','["wood", "fuel", "oil paint", "water"]'::jsonb,1,'Drag costs fuel.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.I',3,'The L/D ratio of a wing is its lift divided by its:','["speed", "thrust", "drag", "weight"]'::jsonb,2,'L/D = lift / drag.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.I',4,'A glider with L/D of 30 travels about how far forward for each unit of altitude lost?','["300 units", "1 unit", "3 units", "30 units"]'::jsonb,3,'L/D approximates glide ratio.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.J',3,'An L/D chart plots the ratio against:','["angle of attack", "price", "passenger weight", "time of day"]'::jsonb,0,'L/D varies with AoA.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.J',4,'The peak of an L/D chart shows the angle with the most:','["stall", "efficient flight", "drag", "turbulence"]'::jsonb,1,'Peak L/D is best efficiency.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.K',3,'Flying at the angle of attack for maximum L/D gives the best:','["weight", "climb rate only", "glide distance", "stall"]'::jsonb,2,'Maximum L/D maximizes range in a glide.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.K',4,'The angle of attack for best glide is found by locating the:','["origin", "edge of the chart", "lowest point of the curve", "peak of the L/D curve"]'::jsonb,3,'Peak L/D gives optimal glide.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.L',3,'A wind turbine blade uses an airfoil shape to produce:','["rotational force", "fog", "sound only", "fuel"]'::jsonb,0,'Lift on the blade turns the rotor.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.L',4,'Besides wings, airfoils are used in propellers, fans, and:','["bridges only", "helicopter rotors", "bricks", "water pipes"]'::jsonb,1,'Rotors are rotating wings.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.M',3,'An unpowered glider can fly because it converts altitude into:','["heat", "thrust from the engine", "forward speed", "fuel"]'::jsonb,2,'Gliding trades height for speed.','original'),
('127.413.d.9.M',4,'A glider can gain altitude by flying in rising air called a:','["dive", "spin", "stall", "thermal"]'::jsonb,3,'Thermals lift gliders.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.A',3,'A moment is a force multiplied by the:','["perpendicular distance from a point", "time", "temperature", "speed"]'::jsonb,0,'Moment = force x distance.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.A',4,'A 500 N lift force acts 0.4 m behind the center of gravity. The moment about the CG is:','["1,250 N-m", "200 N-m", "0.0008 N-m", "20 N-m"]'::jsonb,1,'500 x 0.4 = 200.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.B',3,'The center of gravity of an aircraft is the point where its weight is considered to:','["end", "vanish", "act", "double"]'::jsonb,2,'CG is the balance point.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.B',4,'Loading heavy cargo far behind the CG moves the aircraft''s CG:','["forward", "up only", "nowhere", "aft"]'::jsonb,3,'CG shifts toward added mass.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.C',3,'The center of pressure is the point where total aerodynamic force is considered to:','["act on the wing", "be zero", "double", "vanish"]'::jsonb,0,'CP is the resultant location.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.C',4,'As angle of attack changes, the center of pressure on a cambered wing:','["never moves", "moves", "stays at the wingtip", "disappears"]'::jsonb,1,'CP shifts with AoA.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.D',3,'For stable flight, the center of gravity should generally be located:','["at the wingtip", "behind the center of pressure", "ahead of the center of pressure", "below the runway"]'::jsonb,2,'This gives a restoring nose-down tendency.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_65_v1'
from (values
('127.413.d.10.D',4,'If the CG is moved too far aft, the aircraft becomes:','["slower to build", "more stable", "heavier", "less stable"]'::jsonb,3,'Aft CG reduces stability.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.E',3,'A model aircraft with an adjustable weight lets students test how changing the CG affects:','["stability", "color", "sound", "price"]'::jsonb,0,'Variable CG shows stability effects.','original'),
('127.413.d.10.E',4,'In a model with an adjustable wing position, moving the wing changes the relative locations of the CG and:','["flight plan", "center of pressure", "paint", "control tower"]'::jsonb,1,'Relative position matters.','original'),
('127.413.d.11.A',3,'Computer-aided design software is an engineering tool used to create:','["lunch menus", "paper tickets", "digital models of parts", "schedules only"]'::jsonb,2,'CAD builds models.','original'),
('127.413.d.11.A',4,'Software that simulates airflow over a wing is called:','["presentation software", "word processing software", "spreadsheet software only", "computational fluid dynamics software"]'::jsonb,3,'CFD predicts flow.','original'),
('127.413.d.11.B',3,'In aerospace, simulation tools are used to test designs before:','["building expensive hardware", "learning", "designing", "researching"]'::jsonb,0,'Simulation saves cost.','original'),
('127.413.d.11.B',4,'A benefit of finite element analysis for an airframe is that it shows where the structure is:','["most expensive to paint", "most highly stressed", "most colorful", "most popular"]'::jsonb,1,'FEA reveals stress.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.A',3,'An atmospheric mission requirement might state the aircraft must carry a given:','["logo", "name", "payload", "flag"]'::jsonb,2,'Payload is a core requirement.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.A',4,'A requirement that an aircraft must fly 800 km without refueling is a requirement for:','["name", "color of seats", "color", "range"]'::jsonb,3,'Range is distance per fuel load.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.B',3,'A space mission requirement might specify the target:','["orbit", "uniform", "paint", "logo"]'::jsonb,0,'Orbit defines where the spacecraft goes.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.B',4,'A mission to deliver a satellite to orbit must meet requirements for payload mass and:','["cabin color", "launch vehicle capability", "pilot hobbies", "parking"]'::jsonb,1,'Rockets must be able to lift the payload.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.C',3,'Mission requirements are interrelated because changing one, such as payload mass, can affect:','["only schedule", "nothing else", "other requirements like range or cost", "only color"]'::jsonb,2,'Requirements are linked.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.C',4,'Increasing the required range of an aircraft usually increases its:','["wing color", "name", "noise only", "fuel needs and weight"]'::jsonb,3,'More fuel adds weight.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.D',3,'A mission requirement for a high cruise altitude affects the aircraft''s choice of:','["engine and structure", "name", "seat covers", "paint"]'::jsonb,0,'Altitude drives design.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.D',4,'A spacecraft that must survive radiation will need requirements for its:','["flight attendants", "materials and shielding", "logo", "paint color"]'::jsonb,1,'Environment drives design.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.E',3,'Mission requirements help determine the aircraft''s overall:','["slogan", "logo", "size and configuration", "name"]'::jsonb,2,'They drive the design.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.E',4,'An aircraft that must take off from short runways will need a wing designed for high:','["weight", "price", "top speed only", "lift at low speed"]'::jsonb,3,'Short field operation needs lift.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.F',3,'A mission profile is a graph or diagram showing how the aircraft''s altitude and speed change over:','["the flight", "a year", "the weather", "the factory"]'::jsonb,0,'Profiles trace the mission.','original'),
('127.413.d.12.F',4,'A mission profile with a long cruise at altitude puts emphasis on requirements for:','["hover", "fuel efficiency", "paint", "short takeoff"]'::jsonb,1,'Cruise efficiency matters.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.A',3,'A piston-powered aircraft produces thrust by using the engine to turn a:','["wing", "rocket bell", "propeller", "jet nozzle"]'::jsonb,2,'The propeller accelerates air backward.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.A',4,'Thrust from a propeller is created as the blades push air:','["upward", "sideways", "forward", "backward"]'::jsonb,3,'Reaction pushes the aircraft forward.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.B',3,'In a turboprop, the gas turbine drives the propeller through a:','["reduction gearbox", "landing gear", "wing", "fuel tank"]'::jsonb,0,'The gearbox lowers rotational speed.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.B',4,'Compared with piston engines, turboprops are generally more efficient at:','["zero speed", "medium altitudes and speeds", "very low speeds only", "supersonic speed"]'::jsonb,1,'Turboprops suit regional flights.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.C',3,'A jet engine produces thrust by accelerating a stream of:','["water", "paint", "hot exhaust gas", "wood"]'::jsonb,2,'Exhaust speed produces thrust.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.C',4,'The main sections of a basic turbojet are the compressor, combustor, and:','["wheel and brake", "cabin and seat", "wing and tail", "turbine and nozzle"]'::jsonb,3,'Air is compressed, burned, and expelled.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.D',3,'Unlike a jet engine, a rocket engine carries its own:','["oxidizer", "propeller", "landing gear", "wings"]'::jsonb,0,'Rockets work in space without air.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.D',4,'A rocket can operate in the vacuum of space because it does not need atmospheric:','["gravity", "oxygen", "signals", "light"]'::jsonb,1,'It carries oxidizer.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.E',3,'Solid-fuel rockets are used as boosters because they are:','["delicate", "delicate and reusable only", "simple and can produce high thrust", "unable to be stored"]'::jsonb,2,'Solids are simple and powerful.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.E',4,'A disadvantage of solid-fuel rockets compared with liquid-fuel rockets is that they are hard to:','["store", "paint", "launch", "throttle or shut down"]'::jsonb,3,'Solids burn until used up.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.F',3,'Liquid-fuel rockets can be throttled and restarted, which is useful for:','["precise maneuvers in space", "manual labor", "storage", "painting"]'::jsonb,0,'Liquids allow control.','original'),
('127.413.d.13.F',4,'Liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen are an example of:','["aircraft fuel", "liquid propellants", "battery chemicals", "solid fuel"]'::jsonb,1,'These are common rocket propellants.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.A',3,'Common material classes in aerospace design include metals, polymers, ceramics, and:','["liquids", "gases", "composites", "foods"]'::jsonb,2,'Composites combine materials.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.A',4,'Carbon fiber reinforced polymer is a:','["pure metal", "liquid", "ceramic only", "composite material"]'::jsonb,3,'It combines fibers and resin.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.B',3,'Aluminum alloys have long been used in aircraft skin because they are strong for their:','["low weight", "soft feel", "bright color", "high weight"]'::jsonb,0,'Strength-to-weight ratio is key.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.B',4,'Titanium might be chosen for a jet engine part exposed to high heat because it keeps its:','["color", "strength at high temperature", "softness", "price"]'::jsonb,1,'Titanium handles heat.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.C',3,'Landing gear parts are often made by forging because forging produces parts with high:','["porosity", "transparency", "strength", "softness"]'::jsonb,2,'Forged metal is strong.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.C',4,'A part machined from a solid block of metal is made by:','["knitting", "painting", "pouring liquid", "removing material with cutting tools"]'::jsonb,3,'Machining is subtractive.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.D',3,'Choosing an expensive material for a small part may be justified if it:','["greatly improves performance or safety", "looks nicer only", "is rare", "costs more"]'::jsonb,0,'Cost must be balanced with benefit.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.D',4,'Manufacturing cost affects design decisions because a design that is hard to make may be too:','["light", "expensive to produce", "simple", "strong"]'::jsonb,1,'Manufacturability matters.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.E',3,'If a mission requires a vehicle to survive reentry heat, the material requirements include:','["low strength", "bright color", "high temperature resistance", "low cost only"]'::jsonb,2,'Mission drives materials.','original'),
('127.413.d.14.E',4,'A mission requiring very low weight favors materials with a high:','["hardness only", "density", "cost", "strength-to-weight ratio"]'::jsonb,3,'Light structures need efficient materials.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.A',3,'A truss structure is built from straight members joined into:','["triangles", "circles", "spirals", "spheres"]'::jsonb,0,'Triangles are rigid.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.A',4,'In a monocoque structure, the outer skin carries most of the:','["instruments", "load", "paint", "fuel"]'::jsonb,1,'The skin is the structure.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.B',3,'Semi-monocoque structures use a skin supported by internal frames, which gives a good balance of:','["color and size", "price and name", "strength and weight", "noise and heat"]'::jsonb,2,'Most airliners use semi-monocoque.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.B',4,'Early fabric-covered small aircraft often used a truss fuselage because it was simple and:','["very fragile", "very heavy", "transparent", "easy to build and repair"]'::jsonb,3,'Trusses suit simple aircraft.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.C',3,'A spacecraft that must withstand launch loads and be very light will need a structure chosen to meet its:','["mission requirements", "logo", "uniform", "paint plan"]'::jsonb,0,'Mission drives structure.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.C',4,'A high-pressure cabin at altitude calls for a structure that can handle repeated:','["paint cycles", "pressurization cycles", "lunch breaks", "logo changes"]'::jsonb,1,'Fatigue from pressure cycles matters.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.D',3,'Stringers in a fuselage are long members running along the:','["antenna", "tires", "length of the body", "wings only"]'::jsonb,2,'Stringers stiffen the skin.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.D',4,'Bulkheads and frames in the fuselage help maintain the:','["tail length", "fuel color", "wing area", "cross-sectional shape"]'::jsonb,3,'Frames hold the shape.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.E',3,'Ribs in a wing help maintain the airfoil:','["shape", "color", "name", "price"]'::jsonb,0,'Ribs shape the wing.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.E',4,'The main load-carrying beam running along a wing is the:','["antenna", "spar", "tire", "rib"]'::jsonb,1,'Spars carry bending loads.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.F',3,'Compared with aircraft, spacecraft structures must handle launch loads and a:','["heavy rain", "strong crosswind", "vacuum and temperature extremes", "bird strikes"]'::jsonb,2,'Space is a harsh environment.','original'),
('127.413.d.15.F',4,'A spacecraft structure that is only used in orbit can be lighter than a launch vehicle because it does not carry:','["any mass", "its instruments", "its payload", "aerodynamic loads in flight"]'::jsonb,3,'Orbit has no air drag loads.','original'),
('127.413.d.16.A',3,'The six Keplerian elements describe the size, shape, and orientation of:','["an orbit", "an engine", "a wing", "a hangar"]'::jsonb,0,'Keplerian elements define orbits.','original'),
('127.413.d.16.A',4,'The semi-major axis of an orbit describes the orbit''s:','["color", "size", "age", "speed limit"]'::jsonb,1,'It sets the orbit size.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
